      <tei:div type="colophon" subtype="notes">
        <tei:p>This variation of Barukh shèAmar can be found in Seder Rav Amram (Bodleian Library, shelfmark: Ms. Opp. Add. Qu. 28; catalogue: Neubauer, Oxford, England 1095). The manuscript images are not yet digitized and/or publicly accessible.</tei:p>
        <tei:p>David Hedegard transcribed three manuscript versions of Barukh shèAmar in his dissertation on Seder R. Amram Gaon (Lindstedt, 1951). In his transcription, this source is labeled [א] and in his translation, [O]. I have modifited Hedegard’s translation slightly by re-Hebraizing divine names.</tei:p>
      </tei:div>
